(self.CURRICULUM ||= { lessons: [] }).lessons.push(...[{"n": 34, "title": "Lesson 34: Explanations for Difficult Words", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-34/", "vocab": [{"ko": "흔들다", "en": "to shake", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "흔들거리다", "en": "to be shaking", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "머뭇거리다", "en": "to hesitate", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "반짝거리다", "en": "to be shiny", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "두리번거리다", "en": "to look around (restlessly)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "끈적거리다", "en": "to be sticky", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "출렁거리다", "en": "to slosh, to rock", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "자기", "en": "oneself, him/her", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "자신", "en": "oneself", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "아직", "en": "still, yet", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "여전히", "en": "still", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "별로", "en": "not very", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "전혀", "en": "not at all", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "훨씬", "en": "much more", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "관하다", "en": "to relate to, regarding", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "관련하다", "en": "to be related to", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "주위", "en": "surroundings, around", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일단", "en": "once, first, for now", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "무게", "en": "weight", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "몸무게", "en": "body weight", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "살", "en": "flesh, weight", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "찌다", "en": "to gain weight", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "빠지다", "en": "to come out, fall out (살이 빠지다 = lose weight)", "pos": "verb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~거리다 verbs", "explanation": "Many verbs end in ~거리다. It attaches to a sound or motion root and describes something that happens again and again, or keeps going: shaking, twinkling, hesitating, looking around. The root on its own is usually not a word, so learn each verb as a whole.\n\nThey are ordinary verbs and conjugate like any other. The stem ends in 이, so 이 + 어 contracts to 여: 흔들거리다 → 흔들거리 + 어요 → 흔들거려요. Past: 흔들거리 + 었어요 → 흔들거렸어요. Progressive: 흔들거리고 있어요.\n\nCompare 흔들다 \"to shake (something)\" with 흔들거리다 \"to keep wobbling, keep shaking\". The ~거리다 version paints the repeated movement. You will meet more of these later, such as 북적거리다 (to be bustling) and 후들거리다 (to tremble).", "examples": [{"ko": "배가 계속 흔들거려요.", "en": "The boat keeps rocking.", "note": "흔들거리 + 어요 contracts to 흔들거려요."}, {"ko": "하늘에 별이 반짝거려요.", "en": "Stars are twinkling in the sky."}, {"ko": "그는 대답하기 전에 머뭇거렸어요.", "en": "He hesitated before answering."}, {"ko": "손이 끈적거려서 손을 씻었어요.", "en": "My hands were sticky, so I washed them."}, {"ko": "왜 그렇게 머뭇거려요?", "en": "Why are you hesitating like that?"}, {"ko": "아이가 길에서 두리번거리고 있었어요.", "en": "A child was looking around on the street."}]}, {"point": "자기 and 자신", "explanation": "자기 points back to the subject of the sentence: \"his own\", \"her own\", \"himself\". It is used for a third person (민수, 친구, 그녀). For yourself, use 제 or 내 instead: 저는 제 방을 청소했어요, not 자기 방.\n\n자기 works like a noun. Put it straight before another noun for \"one's own\" (자기 방, 자기 차), or add particles to it (자기를, 자기에게). When English says \"his\" and means the subject's own thing, Korean usually prefers 자기 over 그의.\n\n자신 means \"oneself\". It often follows a person word to stress \"that very person\": 자기 자신 (himself, herself), 제 자신 or 내 자신 (myself), 너 자신 (yourself). On its own it is used for general statements, like 자신을 사랑하다 \"to love oneself\".", "examples": [{"ko": "민수는 자기 방을 청소했어요.", "en": "Minsu cleaned his own room."}, {"ko": "자신을 사랑하는 것은 중요해요.", "en": "It is important to love yourself."}, {"ko": "저는 제 자신을 믿어요.", "en": "I believe in myself."}, {"ko": "그녀는 자기 자신에 대해 이야기했어요.", "en": "She talked about herself."}, {"ko": "친구가 자기 차를 팔았어요.", "en": "My friend sold his own car."}, {"ko": "저는 제 자신이 부끄러웠어요.", "en": "I was ashamed of myself.", "note": "부끄럽다 is ㅂ irregular: 부끄럽 + 었어요 → 부끄러웠어요."}]}, {"point": "아직 vs 여전히 (still)", "explanation": "아직 is about something that has not changed or finished up to now. In a positive sentence it means \"still\": 아직 자고 있어요 \"she's still sleeping\". With a negative it means \"not yet\": 아직 안 왔어요 \"he hasn't come yet\". The short reply 아직이요 means \"not yet\".\n\n여전히 means \"still, as before, as always\". It stresses that something is the same as it was in the past, often when you might expect it to have changed. It sounds a little more formal than 아직, and it never means \"not yet\".\n\nRule of thumb: \"not yet\" or \"hasn't finished up to now\" → 아직. \"Unchanged, same as before\" → 여전히. In many \"still\" sentences either word works.", "examples": [{"ko": "친구가 아직 안 왔어요.", "en": "My friend hasn’t come yet."}, {"ko": "저는 아직 숙제를 하고 있어요.", "en": "I am still doing my homework."}, {"ko": "그는 여전히 바빠요.", "en": "He is still busy (as always)."}, {"ko": "아직 밖이 어두워요.", "en": "It is still dark outside."}, {"ko": "그 영화를 아직 못 봤어요.", "en": "I still haven't been able to see that movie."}, {"ko": "그 식당은 여전히 사람이 많아요.", "en": "That restaurant is still crowded, as always."}]}, {"point": "별로 / 전혀 with negatives", "explanation": "Some adverbs only work in negative sentences. 별로 softens the negative: \"not very\", \"not really\", \"not much\". 전혀 makes it total: \"not at all\", \"never\". Put them before the negative part: 별로 안 비싸요, 전혀 몰라요.\n\nThey pair with any negative: 안 + verb, ~지 않다, 못, and negative words like 없다, 모르다 and 재미없다. Without a negative they sound wrong: say 별로 안 비싸요, never 별로 비싸요.\n\n별로 is also a handy reply on its own. 별로예요 means \"it's not great\", and 별로 안 좋아해요 is a gentle way to say you don't like something. 전혀요 on its own means \"not at all\".", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 별로 배고프지 않아요.", "en": "I’m not very hungry."}, {"ko": "그 영화는 별로 재미없었어요.", "en": "That movie wasn’t really fun."}, {"ko": "저는 고기를 전혀 안 먹어요.", "en": "I don’t eat meat at all."}, {"ko": "그 사람을 전혀 몰라요.", "en": "I don’t know that person at all."}, {"ko": "그 영화 어땠어요? 별로였어요.", "en": "How was the movie? It wasn't great."}, {"ko": "어제는 전혀 못 잤어요.", "en": "I couldn't sleep at all last night."}]}, {"point": "훨씬 (much more)", "explanation": "훨씬 means \"much\" or \"far\" in a comparison. It goes right before the descriptive word and makes the gap bigger: 더 비싸요 \"more expensive\" → 훨씬 비싸요 \"much more expensive\".\n\nThe usual pattern is A이/가 B보다 훨씬 + adjective (lesson 19). You can add 더 for extra stress: 훨씬 더 커요. Without ~보다, the comparison is with something already understood: 이게 훨씬 좋아요 \"this one is much better\".\n\n훨씬 also works with verbs of degree like 잘하다 and 좋아하다. Don't confuse it with 아주 or 너무, which just mean \"very\" and don't compare two things.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘이 어제보다 훨씬 추워요.", "en": "Today is much colder than yesterday."}, {"ko": "기차가 버스보다 훨씬 빨라요.", "en": "The train is much faster than the bus."}, {"ko": "한국어가 영어보다 훨씬 어려워요.", "en": "Korean is much harder than English."}, {"ko": "이 가방이 저 가방보다 훨씬 더 비싸요.", "en": "This bag is much more expensive than that bag."}, {"ko": "지하철로 가는 게 훨씬 빨라요.", "en": "Going by subway is much faster."}, {"ko": "언니가 저보다 요리를 훨씬 잘해요.", "en": "My older sister cooks much better than I do."}]}, {"point": "관하다 / 관련하다 (about, related to)", "explanation": "These words link something to a topic. 관하다 is almost never used on its own; learn its two fixed forms. N에 관한 + noun means \"a (noun) about N\": 역사에 관한 책. N에 관해(서) + verb means \"do something about N\": 문제에 관해 이야기하다. 관한 describes a noun, 관해서 links to a verb.\n\n관련하다 means \"to be related, connected\". Its most common form is N와/과 관련된 + noun, \"a (noun) related to N\". The noun 관련 also appears in 관련이 있다/없다, \"to be related / to have nothing to do with\".\n\nThese are close to N에 대해(서) and N에 대한 from lesson 13, and often interchangeable. 관해서 sounds a bit more formal, so it is common at work and in writing. 관련된 is wider than \"about\": it means \"connected to\".", "examples": [{"ko": "한국 역사에 관한 책을 읽었어요.", "en": "I read a book about Korean history."}, {"ko": "우리는 이 문제에 관해 이야기했어요.", "en": "We talked about this problem."}, {"ko": "저는 환경과 관련된 일을 하고 싶어요.", "en": "I want to do work related to the environment."}, {"ko": "시험에 관해서 질문이 있어요.", "en": "I have a question about the exam."}, {"ko": "여행에 관한 이야기를 듣고 싶어요.", "en": "I want to hear stories about the trip."}, {"ko": "그 일은 저와 관련이 없어요.", "en": "That has nothing to do with me.", "note": "관련 is a noun here: 관련이 있다/없다 means \"to be related / unrelated\"."}]}, {"point": "주위 (around, surroundings)", "explanation": "주위 is a noun meaning \"the area around something\" or \"surroundings\". Put it straight after another noun, no ~의 needed: 집 주위 \"around the house\", 공원 주위 \"around the park\". 이 주위 means \"around here\".\n\nThen add the particle the sentence needs. 주위에 + 있다/없다 says what is around: 집 주위에 나무가 많아요. 주위를 makes it an object, often with 보다 or 두리번거리다: 주위를 봤어요 \"I looked around\".\n\n제 주위 or 주위 사람 means the people around you, such as friends, family and coworkers. The word 주변 is used in almost the same way.", "examples": [{"ko": "집 주위에 나무가 많아요.", "en": "There are many trees around the house."}, {"ko": "제 주위 사람들은 모두 친절해요.", "en": "The people around me are all kind."}, {"ko": "그는 주위를 두리번거렸어요.", "en": "He looked around restlessly."}, {"ko": "공원 주위를 걸었어요.", "en": "I walked around the park."}, {"ko": "이 주위에 편의점이 있어요?", "en": "Is there a convenience store around here?"}, {"ko": "제 주위에는 한국 친구가 없어요.", "en": "I don't have any Korean friends around me."}]}, {"point": "일단 (first, once, for now)", "explanation": "일단 sets up a first step: \"first\", \"once\", \"for now\". Use it when you deal with one thing before worrying about the rest. It usually goes at the start of the sentence.\n\nCommon patterns: 일단 A고 B, \"first A, then B\" (일단 밥을 먹고 공부할 거예요). 일단 A(으)ㄴ 후에 B, \"once A is done, B\". Or 일단 with a single action, \"for now, just ...\" (일단 집에 가요).\n\nThe \"for now\" meaning hints that the plan is temporary and you may decide more later. Compare 먼저, which simply means \"first, before others\" and is about order. 일단 adds the feeling of \"let's start with this and see\".", "examples": [{"ko": "일단 밥을 먹고 공부할 거예요.", "en": "First I will eat, and then study."}, {"ko": "일단 숙제를 끝낸 후에 친구를 만날 거예요.", "en": "Once I finish my homework, I will meet a friend."}, {"ko": "일단 집에 가요.", "en": "Let’s go home for now."}, {"ko": "일단 샤워를 하고 나갈 거예요.", "en": "I'll take a shower first and then go out."}, {"ko": "일단 지금은 괜찮아요.", "en": "For now, I'm okay."}, {"ko": "일단 표를 샀어요. 호텔은 나중에 찾을 거예요.", "en": "I bought the tickets for now. I'll look for a hotel later."}]}, {"point": "Weight words: 무게, 몸무게, 살이 찌다/빠지다", "explanation": "무게 is the weight of a thing. 몸무게 (몸 \"body\" + 무게) is a person's body weight: 제 몸무게는 70킬로예요.\n\nKorean doesn't \"gain\" or \"lose\" 무게. Instead, 살 \"flesh\" is the subject. 살이 찌다 is \"to gain weight\" and 살이 빠지다 is \"to lose weight\". They usually appear in the past, because you describe a change that has already happened: 찌 + 었어요 → 쪘어요, 빠지 + 었어요 → 빠졌어요.\n\nTo talk about trying to lose weight, use 살을 빼다 \"to take off weight\": 살을 빼고 싶어요. 빼다 is something you do on purpose, with 살 as the object. 빠지다 is something that happens, with 살 as the subject.", "examples": [{"ko": "요즘 살이 많이 쪘어요.", "en": "I gained a lot of weight lately."}, {"ko": "운동을 해서 살이 빠졌어요.", "en": "I exercised, so I lost weight."}, {"ko": "이 가방은 무게가 얼마나 돼요?", "en": "How much does this bag weigh?"}, {"ko": "살을 빼고 싶어요.", "en": "I want to lose weight.", "note": "빼다 takes an object (살을), while 빠지다 takes a subject (살이)."}, {"ko": "제 몸무게는 70킬로예요.", "en": "I weigh 70 kilos."}, {"ko": "요즘 살이 빠졌어요?", "en": "Have you lost weight lately?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "아이가 대답하기 전에 ___.", "answer": "머뭇거렸어요", "options": ["머뭇거렸어요", "반짝거렸어요", "끈적거렸어요", "출렁거렸어요"], "en": "The child hesitated before answering.", "why": "머뭇거리다 means to hesitate; the other ~거리다 verbs describe shining, stickiness and sloshing."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "하늘에 별이 ___.", "answer": "반짝거려요", "options": ["반짝거려요", "머뭇거려요", "끈적거려요", "두리번거려요"], "en": "Stars are twinkling in the sky.", "why": "반짝거리다 describes something repeatedly shining or twinkling."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "지수는 ___ 가방을 찾고 있어요.", "answer": "자기", "options": ["자기", "제", "내", "네"], "en": "Jisu is looking for her own bag.", "why": "자기 points back to the third-person subject, 지수."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "버스가 ___ 안 왔어요.", "answer": "아직", "options": ["아직", "여전히", "훨씬", "일단"], "en": "The bus hasn't come yet.", "why": "아직 with a negative means \"not yet\"."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 커피를 ___ 안 마셔요.", "answer": "전혀", "options": ["전혀", "별로", "훨씬", "일단"], "en": "I don't drink coffee at all.", "why": "전혀 with a negative means \"not at all\"; 별로 would only mean \"not much\"."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "지하철이 버스보다 ___ 빨라요.", "answer": "훨씬", "options": ["훨씬", "별로", "전혀", "아직"], "en": "The subway is much faster than the bus.", "why": "훨씬 strengthens a ~보다 comparison; 별로 and 전혀 need a negative."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "한국 음식에 ___ 책을 샀어요.", "answer": "관한", "options": ["관한", "관해", "관하는", "관해서"], "en": "I bought a book about Korean food.", "why": "Before a noun (책) use 관한; 관해(서) goes before a verb."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "요즘 살이 많이 ___.", "answer": "쪘어요", "options": ["쪘어요", "빠졌어요", "찌어요", "쪄었어요"], "en": "I've gained a lot of weight lately.", "why": "살이 찌다 is to gain weight; 찌 + 었어요 contracts to 쪘어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This coffee isn't very hot.", "ko": "이 커피는 별로 뜨겁지 않아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "There are many restaurants around the station.", "ko": "역 주위에 식당이 많아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "For now, I will wait here.", "ko": "일단 여기서 기다릴 거예요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Minsu is still busy (as always).", "ko": "민수는 여전히 바빠요."}], "reading": {"ko": "민수는 요즘 살이 많이 쪘어요. 작년보다 몸무게가 훨씬 많이 나가요. 민수는 일단 매일 공원에서 걷기 시작했어요. 공원 주위에는 나무가 많고 아주 조용해요. 살은 아직 별로 안 빠졌어요. 하지만 민수는 자기 자신을 믿어요. 요즘은 운동에 관한 책도 읽고 있어요.", "en": "Minsu has gained a lot of weight lately. He weighs much more than last year. For now, he has started walking in the park every day. Around the park there are many trees and it is very quiet. He hasn't really lost weight yet. But Minsu believes in himself. These days he is also reading a book about exercise.", "questions": [{"q": "민수는 요즘 살이 쪘어요, 빠졌어요?", "a": "살이 많이 쪘어요."}, {"q": "민수는 매일 어디에서 걸어요?", "a": "공원에서 걸어요."}, {"q": "민수는 무엇에 관한 책을 읽고 있어요?", "a": "운동에 관한 책을 읽고 있어요."}]}}}, {"n": 35, "title": "Lesson 35: It seems to be/might be: 것 같다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-35/", "vocab": [{"ko": "수영장", "en": "swimming pool", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "보건", "en": "public health", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소나무", "en": "pine tree", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "바닥", "en": "floor (ground surface)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "변태", "en": "pervert", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "홍수", "en": "flood", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "새우", "en": "shrimp", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "왕따", "en": "outcast", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "낚시", "en": "fishing", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "후배", "en": "one’s junior", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "꿀", "en": "honey", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "허벅지", "en": "inner thigh", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "말", "en": "horse", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "휴가", "en": "holiday, vacation", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "휴식", "en": "break", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "넘치다", "en": "to overflow", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "피하다", "en": "to avoid", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "펴다", "en": "to unfold, to unroll", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "답장하다", "en": "to respond to a message", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "봉사하다", "en": "to volunteer", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "시도하다", "en": "to try, to attempt", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "펴지다", "en": "to be unfolded, to be unrolled", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "답답하다", "en": "to be stuffy, to be frustrated", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "평화롭다", "en": "to be peaceful", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "신기하다", "en": "to be amazing, to be cool", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "멋있다", "en": "to be stylish", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "오히려", "en": "on the contrary", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~(으)ㄹ 것 같다 (probably will, seems likely)", "explanation": "Use this to guess about something that hasn't happened yet, or something you can't check: \"I think it will\", \"it will probably\". Koreans also use it to sound less blunt, so it is very common. It works with verbs, adjectives and 이다.\n\nForm it with the future modifier ~(으)ㄹ from lesson 26, then 것 같다. Vowel stem: add ~ㄹ (오다 → 올 것 같아요, 바쁘다 → 바쁠 것 같아요). Consonant stem: add ~을 (먹다 → 먹을 것 같아요, 재미있다 → 재미있을 것 같아요). ㄹ stem: no change (알다 → 알 것 같아요). Irregulars follow the usual modifier rules (춥다 → 추울 것 같아요). 이다: 학생일 것 같아요.\n\nTo say \"I don't think it will\", make the verb negative: 안 올 것 같아요 or 오지 않을 것 같아요. In speech 같아요 often sounds like 같애요.", "examples": [{"ko": "내일 비가 올 것 같아요.", "en": "I think it will rain tomorrow."}, {"ko": "이 영화는 재미있을 것 같아요.", "en": "This movie will probably be fun."}, {"ko": "후배가 늦을 것 같아요.", "en": "My junior is probably going to be late."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 새우를 안 먹을 것 같아요.", "en": "I don’t think that person will eat shrimp."}, {"ko": "오늘 밤에 추울 것 같아요.", "en": "I think it will be cold tonight.", "note": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular: 춥 → 추우 + ㄹ → 추울."}, {"ko": "저는 그 답을 알 것 같아요.", "en": "I think I know the answer."}]}, {"point": "~는 / ~(으)ㄴ 것 같다 (it seems that)", "explanation": "Change the modifier before 것 같다 to guess about the present or the past. The choice is the same as when you describe a noun (lesson 26). Present action (verb): ~는, as in 오다 → 오는 것 같아요. Past action (verb): ~(으)ㄴ, as in 가다 → 간 것 같아요, 먹다 → 먹은 것 같아요. Present state (adjective): ~(으)ㄴ, as in 깊다 → 깊은 것 같아요. Words ending in 있다/없다 take ~는: 맛있는 것 같아요.\n\nSo 간 것 같아요 (verb, past) and 깊은 것 같아요 (adjective, present) look alike but differ in time. Check whether the word is a verb or an adjective.\n\nThese forms suggest you are judging from evidence: \"it seems that\". ~았/었을 것 같다 is also about the past, but it is more of a guess without evidence. Compare: 비가 올 것 같아요 (it will probably rain), 비가 오는 것 같아요 (it seems to be raining), 비가 온 것 같아요 (it seems it rained).", "examples": [{"ko": "밖에 비가 오는 것 같아요.", "en": "It seems to be raining outside."}, {"ko": "친구가 벌써 집에 간 것 같아요.", "en": "It seems my friend already went home."}, {"ko": "이 수영장은 아주 깊은 것 같아요.", "en": "This pool seems very deep."}, {"ko": "아빠가 벌써 돈을 냈을 것 같아요.", "en": "Dad has probably already paid."}, {"ko": "동생이 지금 자는 것 같아요.", "en": "I think my younger sibling is sleeping now."}, {"ko": "그 식당은 맛있는 것 같아요.", "en": "That restaurant seems good.", "note": "맛있다 ends in 있다, so it takes ~는, not ~은."}]}, {"point": "그렇다 + 것 같다 (I think so)", "explanation": "그렇다 means \"to be so, to be like that\" (lesson 23). It is an ㅎ irregular adjective, so the ㅎ drops before ~ㄴ and ~ㄹ. 그렇 + ㄴ → 그런 것 같아요, \"I think so / it seems so\". 그렇 + ㄹ → 그럴 것 같아요, \"it will probably be so\". Past: 그랬을 것 같아요, \"it was probably so\".\n\nThese are handy replies when you agree but aren't completely sure. They are softer than a flat 네, 맞아요.\n\nTo disagree politely, make it negative: 그렇지 않은 것 같아요, \"I don't think so\". 안 그런 것 같아요 means the same and is more casual. For the future: 안 그럴 것 같아요.", "examples": [{"ko": "네, 그런 것 같아요.", "en": "Yes, I think so."}, {"ko": "아마 그럴 것 같아요.", "en": "It will probably be like that."}, {"ko": "아니요, 그렇지 않은 것 같아요.", "en": "No, I don’t think so."}, {"ko": "안 그런 것 같아요.", "en": "I don't think so."}, {"ko": "내일도 그럴 것 같아요?", "en": "Do you think it will be like that tomorrow too?"}, {"ko": "옛날에도 그랬을 것 같아요.", "en": "It was probably like that in the old days too."}]}, {"point": "~겠다 for guesses", "explanation": "In lesson 5 you met ~겠 as a future marker. It also expresses a guess based on what you see or hear right now: \"it must be\", \"that looks\", \"that sounds\". It is very common as a reaction to someone's news or to something in front of you.\n\nForm: stem + 겠어요, and the stem never changes, even for irregulars. 맛있다 → 맛있겠어요 \"that must be tasty\". 피곤하다 → 피곤하겠어요 \"you must be tired\". 춥다 → 춥겠어요. 좋다 → 좋겠어요 \"that'll be nice / lucky you\". For a guess about the past, add ~았/었 first: 힘들다 → 힘들었겠어요 \"that must have been hard\".\n\nCompare ~(으)ㄹ 것 같다: both are guesses, but ~겠어요 is a quick reaction in the moment, while ~(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 sounds more like a thought-out opinion. With \"I\" as the subject, ~겠어요 can still mean \"I will\" (lesson 5); context tells you which.", "examples": [{"ko": "와, 이 꿀이 정말 맛있겠어요!", "en": "Wow, this honey must be really delicious!"}, {"ko": "하루 종일 일해서 피곤하겠어요.", "en": "You worked all day, so you must be tired."}, {"ko": "그 낚시 여행은 정말 재미있겠어요.", "en": "That fishing trip sounds like it will be really fun."}, {"ko": "와, 좋겠어요!", "en": "Wow, lucky you!"}, {"ko": "어제 많이 힘들었겠어요.", "en": "Yesterday must have been really hard for you."}, {"ko": "밖이 많이 춥겠어요.", "en": "It must be really cold outside."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "하늘이 어두워요. 오후에 비가 ___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "올", "options": ["올", "오는", "온", "와"], "en": "The sky is dark. I think it will rain this afternoon.", "why": "For a guess about the future, use the ~(으)ㄹ modifier before 것 같다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 영화는 재미___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "있을", "options": ["있을", "있는", "있은", "있울"], "en": "This movie will probably be fun (I haven't seen it yet).", "why": "A guess about something not yet experienced uses ~(으)ㄹ 것 같다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "밖에 눈이 ___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "오는", "options": ["오는", "온", "올", "와"], "en": "It seems to be snowing outside right now.", "why": "A present action you judge from evidence takes ~는 것 같다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "민수가 벌써 휴가를 ___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "간", "options": ["간", "가는", "갈", "가"], "en": "It seems Minsu has already gone on vacation.", "why": "A past action takes the ~(으)ㄴ modifier before 것 같다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 방은 너무 ___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "답답한", "options": ["답답한", "답답하는", "답답할", "답답해"], "en": "This room seems really stuffy.", "why": "A present-tense adjective takes ~(으)ㄴ before 것 같다, never ~는."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저 식당이 비싸요? — 네, ___ 것 같아요.", "answer": "그런", "options": ["그런", "그렇는", "그렇은", "그렇게"], "en": "Is that restaurant expensive? — Yes, it seems so.", "why": "그렇다 becomes 그런 before 것 같다 (\"it seems so\")."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "하루 종일 일했어요? 정말 ___.", "answer": "피곤하겠어요", "options": ["피곤하겠어요", "피곤하겠요", "피곤하게요", "피곤하겠어서"], "en": "You worked all day? You must be really tired.", "why": "~겠어요 expresses a guess about someone else's state."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "와, 이 새우가 정말 ___!", "answer": "맛있겠어요", "options": ["맛있겠어요", "맛있겠요", "맛있게요", "맛있게어요"], "en": "Wow, this shrimp must be really delicious!", "why": "~겠어요 reacts to what you see with a guess: \"it must be\"."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I think it will be peaceful there.", "ko": "거기는 평화로울 것 같아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That person seems really stylish.", "ko": "그 사람은 정말 멋있는 것 같아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "It seems he already replied.", "ko": "그 사람이 벌써 답장한 것 같아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That must be really amazing!", "ko": "정말 신기하겠어요!"}], "reading": {"ko": "다음 주부터 휴가예요. 저는 친구하고 바다에 갈 거예요. 거기는 아주 평화로울 것 같아요. 친구는 낚시를 좋아하고 저는 새우를 좋아해요. 하지만 다음 주에 비가 올 것 같아요. 그래도 괜찮을 것 같아요. 친구도 이 여행을 아주 기대하는 것 같아요.", "en": "My vacation starts next week. I'm going to the sea with a friend. I think it will be very peaceful there. My friend likes fishing and I like shrimp. But it looks like it will rain next week. Still, I think it will be okay. My friend seems to be really looking forward to this trip too.", "questions": [{"q": "이 사람은 누구하고 바다에 갈 거예요?", "a": "친구하고 갈 거예요."}, {"q": "친구는 무엇을 좋아해요?", "a": "낚시를 좋아해요."}, {"q": "다음 주 날씨는 어떨 것 같아요?", "a": "비가 올 것 같아요."}]}}}, {"n": 36, "title": "Lesson 36: To look like: 보이다 / ~아/어 보이다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-36/", "vocab": [{"ko": "소방서", "en": "fire station", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "무릎", "en": "knee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "얼음", "en": "ice", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "흡연", "en": "smoking", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "종아리", "en": "calf", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "관리비", "en": "maintenance fee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "치료비", "en": "medical fee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "보관료", "en": "storage fee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "땅값", "en": "land price", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "입장료", "en": "entrance fee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "등록금", "en": "tuition fee", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교통비", "en": "transportation cost", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "원룸", "en": "studio apartment", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "화장", "en": "makeup", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소방", "en": "firefighting", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "초등학교", "en": "elementary school", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "첫차", "en": "first bus/train", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가격표", "en": "price tag", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여우", "en": "fox", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "의욕", "en": "drive, motivation", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일방적", "en": "one-sided", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이사하다", "en": "to move to a new house", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "귀국하다", "en": "to return home (country)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "터지다", "en": "to explode", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "기대다", "en": "to lean against", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "얻다", "en": "to gain, to get, to obtain", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "임신하다", "en": "to get pregnant", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "보이다", "en": "to be seen", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "들리다", "en": "to be heard", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "짜다", "en": "to be salty", "pos": "adjective"}], "grammar": [{"point": "N 같이 보이다 (to look like N)", "explanation": "보이다 means \"to be seen\", so a sentence with 보여요 literally says \"X is seen as ...\". English usually says \"X looks ...\" instead.\n\nTo say something looks like a noun, put 같이 \"like\" right after the noun, then 보이다: 여우같이 보여요 \"it looks like a fox\". The thing being described takes ~이/가 or ~은/는. 같이 is written attached to the noun, with no space.\n\nOnly 보이다 is conjugated. Its stem ends in 이, so 이 + 어 contracts: 보이 + 어요 → 보여요. Past: 보였어요. Casual: 보여. N처럼 보이다 means the same thing.", "examples": [{"ko": "너는 선생님같이 보여.", "en": "You look like a teacher."}, {"ko": "저 구름은 여우같이 보여요.", "en": "That cloud looks like a fox."}, {"ko": "그 남자는 연예인같이 보여요.", "en": "That man looks like a celebrity."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 학생같이 보였어요.", "en": "That person looked like a student."}, {"ko": "이 꽃은 진짜같이 보여요.", "en": "This flower looks real."}, {"ko": "제가 바보같이 보여요?", "en": "Do I look like a fool?"}]}, {"point": "보이다 / 들리다 (can see / can hear)", "explanation": "보이다 \"to be seen\" and 들리다 \"to be heard\" describe what reaches your eyes or ears without effort. English says \"I can see\" or \"I can hear\", but in Korean the thing seen or heard is the subject with ~이/가, and \"I\" is usually dropped: 산이 보여요, literally \"the mountain is seen\".\n\nCompare the active verbs. 보다 \"to look at, watch\" and 듣다 \"to listen to\" take an object: 산을 봐요, 음악을 들어요. 보이다 and 들리다 take a subject: 산이 보여요, 음악이 들려요. A common mistake is 산을 보여요.\n\nConjugation: 보이 + 어요 → 보여요, 들리 + 어요 → 들려요. The negative normally uses 안: 안 보여요 \"I can't see it\", 잘 안 들려요 \"I can't hear it well\".", "examples": [{"ko": "여기서 산이 안 보여요.", "en": "I can’t see the mountain from here."}, {"ko": "소방서가 보여요?", "en": "Can you see the fire station?"}, {"ko": "네 목소리가 잘 안 들려.", "en": "I can’t hear your voice well."}, {"ko": "밖에서 이상한 소리가 들렸어요.", "en": "I heard a strange sound outside."}, {"ko": "음악 소리가 들려요?", "en": "Can you hear the music?"}, {"ko": "창문으로 바다가 보였어요.", "en": "I could see the sea through the window."}]}, {"point": "N 맛 같다 / N 냄새 같다 (tastes/smells like)", "explanation": "맛 means \"taste\" and 냄새 means \"smell\". To say something tastes or smells like X, put X before 맛 or 냄새 and finish with 같다: 딸기 맛 같아요 \"it tastes like strawberry\", 장미 냄새 같아요 \"it smells like roses\".\n\n같다 is an adjective (lesson 15), so conjugate it normally: 같 + 아요 → 같아요, past 같았어요. To ask \"what does it taste like?\", use 무슨: 무슨 맛 같아요?\n\nA related pattern is very common: N 맛이 나다 / N 냄새가 나다, \"a taste/smell of N comes out\". 김치 냄새가 나요 \"it smells of kimchi\". The 같다 version compares (\"like N\"), while 나다 says the taste or smell is really there.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 음식은 김치 맛 같아요.", "en": "This food tastes like kimchi."}, {"ko": "이 사탕은 딸기 맛 같아요.", "en": "This candy tastes like strawberry."}, {"ko": "이 향수는 장미 냄새 같아요.", "en": "This perfume smells like roses."}, {"ko": "이 차는 꿀 맛 같아요.", "en": "This tea tastes like honey."}, {"ko": "이거 무슨 냄새 같아요?", "en": "What do you think this smells like?"}, {"ko": "방에서 이상한 냄새가 나요.", "en": "There's a strange smell in the room."}]}, {"point": "Adjective + ~아/어 보이다 (to look + adjective)", "explanation": "Attach ~아/어 보이다 to an adjective stem to say how something looks from the outside: \"looks tired\", \"looks salty\". You are judging only by appearance. Only 보이다 at the end takes tense and politeness.\n\nForm the first part like the polite present without 요. Last vowel ㅏ or ㅗ → ~아: 좋다 → 좋아 보여요. Other vowels → ~어: 맛있다 → 맛있어 보여요. 하다 → 해: 피곤하다 → 피곤해 보여요. Contractions and irregulars apply: 짜다 → 짜 보여요, 바쁘다 → 바빠 보여요, 어렵다 → 어려워 보여요.\n\nCompare N 같이 보이다: a noun before 같이 means \"looks like a (noun)\", while an adjective with ~아/어 보이다 means \"looks (adjective)\". Don't mix this up with ~아/어 보다 \"try doing\" from lesson 32: 먹어 봐요 is \"try eating\", not \"looks edible\".", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘 피곤해 보여요.", "en": "You look tired today."}, {"ko": "그 음식이 짜 보여요.", "en": "That food looks salty."}, {"ko": "이 원룸은 아주 좋아 보여요.", "en": "This studio looks very nice."}, {"ko": "동생이 행복해 보였어요.", "en": "My younger sibling looked happy."}, {"ko": "이 문제는 어려워 보여요.", "en": "This problem looks difficult.", "note": "어렵다 is ㅂ irregular: 어렵 + 어 → 어려워."}, {"ko": "그 가방은 별로 비싸 보이지 않아요.", "en": "That bag doesn't look very expensive."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "민수 씨, 오늘 기분이 ___ 보여요.", "answer": "좋아", "options": ["좋아", "좋", "좋은", "좋고"], "en": "Minsu, you look like you're in a good mood today.", "why": "An adjective takes its ~아/어 form before 보이다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 김치가 좀 ___ 보여요.", "answer": "짜", "options": ["짜", "짠", "짜고", "짜다"], "en": "This kimchi looks a bit salty.", "why": "짜다 + ~아 보이다 contracts to 짜 보이다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 아이가 아주 ___ 보였어요.", "answer": "행복해", "options": ["행복해", "행복한", "행복하", "행복하고"], "en": "That child looked very happy.", "why": "하다 adjectives become ~해 before 보이다; the tense goes on 보이다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저 구름은 고양이___ 보여요.", "answer": "같이", "options": ["같이", "같은", "같아", "같고"], "en": "That cloud looks like a cat.", "why": "Noun + 같이 + 보이다 means \"to look like\" that noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "창문으로 소방서가 ___.", "answer": "보여요", "options": ["보여요", "봐요", "들려요", "보아요"], "en": "I can see the fire station through the window.", "why": "보이다 (\"to be seen\") takes the thing seen as its subject: 소방서가 보여요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "옆 방에서 음악 소리가 ___.", "answer": "들려요", "options": ["들려요", "들어요", "보여요", "듣어요"], "en": "I can hear music from the next room.", "why": "With 소리가 as the subject, use 들리다 (\"to be heard\"), not 듣다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "음악___ 잘 안 들려요.", "answer": "이", "options": ["이", "을", "에", "으로"], "en": "I can't hear the music well.", "why": "The thing heard is the subject of 들리다, so it takes 이/가."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 음료수는 딸기 ___ 같아요.", "answer": "맛", "options": ["맛", "냄새", "색", "소리"], "en": "This drink tastes like strawberry.", "why": "Noun + 맛 같다 means \"tastes like\" that noun."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This studio apartment looks small.", "ko": "이 원룸은 작아 보여요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That bag looks heavy.", "ko": "그 가방은 무거워 보여요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "That man looks like a student.", "ko": "그 남자는 학생같이 보여요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "This smells like coffee.", "ko": "이거 커피 냄새 같아요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 오늘 새 원룸을 보러 갔어요. 원룸은 좀 작아 보였어요. 하지만 아주 깨끗해 보였어요. 창문으로 산이 보였어요. 밖에서 차 소리가 조금 들렸어요. 관리비는 조금 비싼 것 같아요. 하지만 저는 그 원룸이 마음에 들어요.", "en": "Today I went to see a new studio apartment. The studio looked a little small. But it looked very clean. I could see a mountain through the window. I could hear a little car noise from outside. The maintenance fee seems a bit expensive. But I like that studio.", "questions": [{"q": "원룸은 어때 보였어요?", "a": "좀 작아 보였어요. 하지만 아주 깨끗해 보였어요."}, {"q": "창문으로 뭐가 보였어요?", "a": "산이 보였어요."}, {"q": "밖에서 무슨 소리가 들렸어요?", "a": "차 소리가 들렸어요."}]}}}, {"n": 37, "title": "Lesson 37: Because: ~아/어서", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-37/", "vocab": [{"ko": "도로", "en": "road", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "고속도로", "en": "highway", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "스님", "en": "Buddhist monk", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "당국", "en": "authorities", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "도시락", "en": "lunch box", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여행자", "en": "traveler", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "피해", "en": "damage", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "성형", "en": "plastic surgery", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "연휴", "en": "continuous holidays", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "최신", "en": "the latest", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "진심", "en": "sincerity, truth", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "한편", "en": "meanwhile", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "반면", "en": "on the other hand (in contrast)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "점프하다", "en": "to jump", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "깜빡하다", "en": "to forget (briefly)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "빨다", "en": "to suck", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "뜨다", "en": "to open one’s eyes", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "감다", "en": "to close one’s eyes", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "다투다", "en": "to fight verbally", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "겨루다", "en": "to compete, to vie", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "개설하다", "en": "to establish, to open", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "설레다", "en": "to be excited, heart fluttering", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "떠지다", "en": "to have one’s eyes open", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "감기다", "en": "to have one’s eyes closed", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "왜냐하면", "en": "because", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "그래서", "en": "therefore", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~아/어서 (because, so)", "explanation": "~아/어서 links two clauses: the first is the reason, the second is the result. 배가 고파서 먹었어요, \"I was hungry, so I ate.\" It is the most common way to give a reason in everyday speech, and the normal way to say what you are sorry or thankful for: 늦어서 미안해요.\n\nForm it like the polite present, with ~서 instead of ~요. ㅏ/ㅗ stems → ~아서: 많다 → 많아서, 가다 → 가서. Other vowels → ~어서: 먹다 → 먹어서, 막히다 → 막혀서. 하다 → 해서: 피곤하다 → 피곤해서. Irregulars apply: 고프다 → 고파서, 춥다 → 추워서, 모르다 → 몰라서.\n\nYou met ~아/어서 in lesson 17 for actions in sequence (가서 먹어요). The \"because\" meaning uses the same ending; context tells you which. One rule: the second clause can't be a command or a suggestion.", "examples": [{"ko": "배가 고파서 도시락을 먹었어요.", "en": "I was hungry, so I ate my lunch box.", "note": "고프다 drops ㅡ: 고프 + 아서 → 고파서."}, {"ko": "도로가 막혀서 늦었어요.", "en": "The road was jammed, so I was late."}, {"ko": "너무 설레서 잠을 못 잤어요.", "en": "I was so excited that I couldn’t sleep."}, {"ko": "날씨가 추워서 코트를 입었어요.", "en": "It was cold, so I wore a coat."}, {"ko": "늦어서 미안해요.", "en": "Sorry I'm late."}, {"ko": "길을 몰라서 친구에게 물어봤어요.", "en": "I didn't know the way, so I asked a friend.", "note": "모르다 is 르 irregular: 모르 + 아서 → 몰라서."}]}, {"point": "Tense with ~아/어서", "explanation": "Never put ~았/었 or ~겠 before ~아/어서. The reason clause has no tense of its own; it borrows the tense of the final verb.\n\nPast: 비가 와서 집에 있었어요, \"it rained, so I stayed home\". Present: 일이 많아서 피곤해요, \"I have a lot of work, so I'm tired\". Future: 내일은 바빠서 못 갈 거예요, \"I'll be busy, so I won't be able to go\". The reason clause looks the same each time.\n\nA common mistake is 비가 왔어서 or 배가 고팠어서. Always use the plain form: 와서, 고파서. This is one of the main differences from ~기 때문에 (next lesson), which can take ~았/었.", "examples": [{"ko": "어제 비가 와서 집에 있었어요.", "en": "It rained yesterday, so I stayed home."}, {"ko": "내일은 바빠서 못 갈 거예요.", "en": "I’ll be busy tomorrow, so I won’t be able to go."}, {"ko": "숙제를 깜빡해서 선생님이 화가 났어요.", "en": "I forgot my homework, so the teacher got angry."}, {"ko": "요즘 일이 많아서 피곤해요.", "en": "I have a lot of work these days, so I'm tired."}, {"ko": "다음 주에 시험이 있어서 이번 주말에 공부할 거예요.", "en": "I have an exam next week, so I'll study this weekend."}, {"ko": "어제 늦게 일어나서 수업에 늦었어요.", "en": "I got up late yesterday, so I was late for class.", "note": "Not 일어났어서: the past shows only on 늦었어요."}]}, {"point": "Noun + (이)라서 (because it is N)", "explanation": "이다 has its own reason form, (이)라서, meaning \"because it is N\". Noun ends in a consonant → 이라서: 학생 → 학생이라서. Noun ends in a vowel → 라서: 연휴 → 연휴라서.\n\nThe negative 아니다 works the same way: N이/가 아니라서, \"because it isn't N\".\n\nYou may also hear 이어서/여서 (학생이어서, 의사여서). They mean the same; (이)라서 is more common in speech. As with ~아/어서, there is no past marker before it: 어제는 일요일이라서 쉬었어요.", "examples": [{"ko": "연휴라서 고속도로가 막혀요.", "en": "It’s a long holiday, so the highway is jammed."}, {"ko": "저는 학생이라서 돈이 별로 없어요.", "en": "I’m a student, so I don’t have much money."}, {"ko": "그 사람은 스님이라서 고기를 안 먹어요.", "en": "He is a monk, so he doesn’t eat meat."}, {"ko": "최신 핸드폰이 아니라서 느려요.", "en": "It’s not the latest phone, so it’s slow."}, {"ko": "어제는 일요일이라서 늦게 일어났어요.", "en": "Yesterday was Sunday, so I got up late."}, {"ko": "처음이라서 잘 몰라요.", "en": "It's my first time, so I don't really know."}]}, {"point": "그래서 (so, therefore)", "explanation": "그래서 starts a sentence and shows that it is the result of the sentence before: \"so\", \"that's why\". It comes from 그렇다 + ~아/어서, \"being like that\", so it is the sentence-starting version of ~아/어서.\n\nTwo sentences with 그래서 say the same thing as one sentence with ~아/어서: 피곤했어요. 그래서 일찍 잤어요. = 피곤해서 일찍 잤어요. Notice that in the 그래서 version, the first sentence keeps its own past tense (피곤했어요), because it is a complete sentence.\n\nIn conversation, 그래서요? or 그래서 어떻게 됐어요? asks someone to go on: \"And so? What happened?\"", "examples": [{"ko": "어제 너무 피곤했어요. 그래서 일찍 잤어요.", "en": "I was very tired yesterday. So I went to bed early."}, {"ko": "비가 많이 왔어요. 그래서 집에 있었어요.", "en": "It rained a lot. So I stayed home."}, {"ko": "도시락을 깜빡했어요. 그래서 점심을 못 먹었어요.", "en": "I forgot my lunch box. So I couldn’t eat lunch."}, {"ko": "저는 한국 음식을 좋아해요. 그래서 한국어를 배우고 있어요.", "en": "I like Korean food. That's why I'm learning Korean."}, {"ko": "그래서 어떻게 됐어요?", "en": "So what happened?"}, {"ko": "내일 시험이 있어요. 그래서 오늘 밤에 공부할 거예요.", "en": "I have an exam tomorrow. So I'll study tonight."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "배가 너무 ___ 라면을 두 개 먹었어요.", "answer": "고파서", "options": ["고파서", "고프서", "고팠어서", "고프고서"], "en": "I was so hungry that I ate two packs of ramen.", "why": "고프다 is ㅡ irregular, so it becomes 고파서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "어제 눈이 많이 ___ 학교에 안 갔어요.", "answer": "와서", "options": ["와서", "왔어서", "오서", "올서"], "en": "It snowed a lot yesterday, so I didn't go to school.", "why": "Never put the past tense before ~아/어서; the final verb carries it."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "다리가 ___ 택시를 탔어요.", "answer": "아파서", "options": ["아파서", "아프서", "아팠어서", "아퍼서"], "en": "My legs hurt, so I took a taxi.", "why": "아프다 is ㅡ irregular: 아프 + 아서 becomes 아파서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "연휴___ 서울역에 사람이 많아요.", "answer": "라서", "options": ["라서", "이라서", "에서", "가서"], "en": "It's a long holiday, so Seoul Station is crowded.", "why": "연휴 ends in a vowel, so it takes 라서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "오늘은 일요일___ 학교에 안 가요.", "answer": "이라서", "options": ["이라서", "라서", "에서", "으로서"], "en": "Today is Sunday, so I'm not going to school.", "why": "일요일 ends in a consonant, so it takes 이라서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "오늘은 일이 너무 많았어요. ___ 저녁을 못 먹었어요.", "answer": "그래서", "options": ["그래서", "하지만", "왜냐하면", "그런데"], "en": "I had too much work today. So I couldn't eat dinner.", "why": "그래서 starts a sentence that is the result of the one before."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "날씨가 ___ 창문을 열었어요.", "answer": "더워서", "options": ["더워서", "덥어서", "더웠어서", "덥서"], "en": "It was hot, so I opened the window.", "why": "덥다 is ㅂ irregular: 덥 + 어서 becomes 더워서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "친구 이름을 ___ 너무 미안했어요.", "answer": "깜빡해서", "options": ["깜빡해서", "깜빡했어서", "깜빡하서", "깜빡해고"], "en": "I forgot my friend's name, so I felt really sorry.", "why": "하다 verbs become ~해서, with no past tense before it."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The long holiday starts tomorrow, so I'm really excited.", "ko": "내일부터 연휴라서 정말 설레요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I was busy, so I couldn't call.", "ko": "바빠서 전화를 못 했어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "It's not my bag, so I don't know.", "ko": "제 가방이 아니라서 몰라요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I was sick yesterday. So I didn't go to school.", "ko": "어제 아팠어요. 그래서 학교에 안 갔어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "지난 주말은 연휴라서 가족하고 부산에 갔어요. 고속도로가 너무 막혀서 다섯 시간이 걸렸어요. 저는 도시락을 깜빡했어요. 그래서 배가 많이 고팠어요. 부산에서 바다를 봤어요. 바다가 너무 예뻐서 기분이 좋았어요.", "en": "Last weekend was a long holiday, so I went to Busan with my family. The highway was so jammed that it took five hours. I forgot my lunch box. So I was very hungry. In Busan I saw the sea. The sea was so pretty that I felt good.", "questions": [{"q": "고속도로가 어땠어요?", "a": "너무 막혔어요."}, {"q": "왜 배가 고팠어요?", "a": "도시락을 깜빡해서 배가 고팠어요."}, {"q": "바다를 보고 기분이 어땠어요?", "a": "기분이 좋았어요."}]}}}, {"n": 38, "title": "Lesson 38: Because: ~기 때문에", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-38/", "vocab": [{"ko": "투자자", "en": "investor", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "주식", "en": "stocks", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "주식시장", "en": "stock market", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교복", "en": "school uniform", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "여신", "en": "goddess", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "별명", "en": "nickname", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기업", "en": "enterprise", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "현실", "en": "reality", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "폼", "en": "posture", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "제사", "en": "ancestral memorial rite", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "떨어뜨리다", "en": "to drop", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "제안하다", "en": "to propose, to suggest", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "투자하다", "en": "to invest", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "부정하다", "en": "to deny", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "되짚다", "en": "to look back, retrace", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "망설이다", "en": "to hesitate", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "세수하다", "en": "to wash one’s face", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "통화하다", "en": "to talk on the phone", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "떨어지다", "en": "to fall, to drop", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "북적거리다", "en": "to be crowded, busy", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "귀찮다", "en": "to be annoying", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "고르게", "en": "evenly, flatly", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "N 때문에 (because of N)", "explanation": "때문 is a noun meaning \"reason, cause\". It never stands alone. Put it after a noun and add ~에 to say \"because of N\": 비 때문에 \"because of the rain\".\n\nThe noun doesn't change, whether it ends in a vowel or a consonant: 비 때문에, 시험 때문에. People work too: 너 때문에, 친구 때문에. To end a sentence, use N 때문이에요, \"it's because of N\".\n\nN 때문에 often sounds like blame. For a good cause, 덕분에 \"thanks to\" is more natural: 친구 덕분에. Also note that N 때문에 is for nouns only; verbs and adjectives need ~기 때문에 (next point).", "examples": [{"ko": "비 때문에 여행을 못 갔어요.", "en": "I couldn’t go on the trip because of the rain."}, {"ko": "너 때문에 늦었어.", "en": "I was late because of you."}, {"ko": "시험 때문에 요즘 바빠요.", "en": "I’m busy these days because of exams."}, {"ko": "감기 때문에 학교에 못 갔어요.", "en": "I couldn't go to school because of a cold."}, {"ko": "동생 때문에 화가 났어요.", "en": "I got angry because of my younger sibling."}, {"ko": "다 저 때문이에요.", "en": "It's all because of me."}]}, {"point": "~기 때문에 (because)", "explanation": "~기 turns a verb or adjective into a noun (lesson 29). Add 때문에 and you get \"because (it does / it is)\": 바쁘다 → 바쁘 + 기 때문에 → 바쁘기 때문에.\n\nThe stem never changes before ~기, so there are no vowel or irregular rules: 먹다 → 먹기 때문에, 어렵다 → 어렵기 때문에, 모르다 → 모르기 때문에. With 이다, attach it to the noun: 학생이기 때문에.\n\n~기 때문에 means the same as ~아/어서, but it sounds firmer and a bit more formal, so it suits writing and explanations. In chat ~아/어서 is more natural, and for apologies and thanks you need ~아/어서: 늦어서 미안해요, not 늦기 때문에 미안해요. Like ~아/어서, the next clause should not be a command or a suggestion.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘 바쁘기 때문에 못 만나요.", "en": "I can’t meet today because I’m busy."}, {"ko": "거리가 북적거리기 때문에 집에 있을 거예요.", "en": "I’ll stay home because the streets are crowded."}, {"ko": "그는 학생이기 때문에 교복을 입어요.", "en": "He wears a school uniform because he is a student."}, {"ko": "이 책은 어렵기 때문에 천천히 읽어요.", "en": "This book is difficult, so I read it slowly."}, {"ko": "저는 고기를 안 먹기 때문에 채소만 먹어요.", "en": "I don't eat meat, so I only eat vegetables."}, {"ko": "길을 모르기 때문에 지도를 봤어요.", "en": "I didn't know the way, so I looked at a map."}]}, {"point": "~았/었기 때문에 and ~(으)ㄹ 것이기 때문에", "explanation": "Unlike ~아/어서, ~기 때문에 can carry its own tense. This lets you show that the reason happened at a different time from the result.\n\nPast reason: put ~았/었 on the stem, then 기 때문에. 자다 → 잤기 때문에, 먹다 → 먹었기 때문에, 하다 → 했기 때문에. Future reason: ~(으)ㄹ 것 + 이기 때문에. 오다 → 올 것이기 때문에, 많다 → 많을 것이기 때문에. In speech 것이 often shrinks to 거: 올 거기 때문에.\n\nCompare: 어제 늦게 잤기 때문에 피곤해요 has a past reason and a present result. With ~아/어서 you'd say 어제 늦게 자서 피곤해요: no past marker on 자서, and 어제 makes the time clear.", "examples": [{"ko": "주식에 투자했기 때문에 돈을 벌었어요.", "en": "I made money because I invested in stocks."}, {"ko": "어제 늦게 잤기 때문에 피곤해요.", "en": "I’m tired because I went to bed late yesterday."}, {"ko": "내일 비가 올 것이기 때문에 우산을 가져갈 거예요.", "en": "I will take an umbrella because it will rain tomorrow."}, {"ko": "열심히 공부했기 때문에 시험을 잘 봤어요.", "en": "I studied hard, so I did well on the exam."}, {"ko": "돈이 없었기 때문에 아무것도 못 샀어요.", "en": "I had no money, so I couldn't buy anything."}, {"ko": "주말에는 사람이 많을 것이기 때문에 일찍 갈 거예요.", "en": "There will be a lot of people on the weekend, so I'll go early."}]}, {"point": "~기 때문이다 and 그렇기 때문에", "explanation": "To put the reason at the end, finish the sentence with ~기 때문이에요, \"it's because ...\". This is natural when the reason answers a question, or after 이유는 \"the reason is\". For a past reason add ~았/었: 아팠기 때문이에요. With a noun, use N 때문이에요.\n\n왜냐하면 \"because\" opens a reason sentence, and ~기 때문이에요 usually closes it. They work as a pair: 왜냐하면 내일 시험이 있기 때문이에요.\n\n그렇기 때문에 is 그렇다 + ~기 때문에, used at the start of a sentence: \"because of that\", \"therefore\". It is like 그래서 but more formal and emphatic, so you'll see it in writing and speeches.", "examples": [{"ko": "제가 늦은 이유는 길이 막혔기 때문이에요.", "en": "The reason I was late is that the road was jammed."}, {"ko": "왜냐하면 너무 피곤하기 때문이에요.", "en": "Because I’m too tired."}, {"ko": "비가 많이 와요. 그렇기 때문에 밖에 안 나갈 거예요.", "en": "It’s raining a lot. Therefore, I won’t go out."}, {"ko": "어제 못 간 것은 아팠기 때문이에요.", "en": "The reason I couldn't go yesterday is that I was sick."}, {"ko": "오늘은 일찍 잘 거예요. 왜냐하면 내일 시험이 있기 때문이에요.", "en": "I'll go to bed early today. Because I have an exam tomorrow."}, {"ko": "그 영화는 정말 유명해요. 그렇기 때문에 표가 없어요.", "en": "That movie is really famous. That's why there are no tickets."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "눈 ___ 학교에 못 갔어요.", "answer": "때문에", "options": ["때문에", "때문이에요", "때문을", "때문이"], "en": "I couldn't go to school because of the snow.", "why": "Noun + 때문에 means \"because of\" that noun."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 시험이 ___ 때문에 오늘은 공부할 거예요.", "answer": "있기", "options": ["있기", "있어서", "있는", "있게"], "en": "Because I have an exam tomorrow, I'll study today.", "why": "A verb or adjective needs ~기 before 때문에."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "어젯밤에 잠을 못 ___ 때문에 너무 피곤해요.", "answer": "잤기", "options": ["잤기", "자기", "잔", "잘"], "en": "I'm very tired because I couldn't sleep last night.", "why": "~기 때문에 can carry its own past tense: 잤기 때문에."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 동생은 학생___ 때문에 교복을 입어요.", "answer": "이기", "options": ["이기", "기", "인", "이에"], "en": "My younger sibling wears a school uniform because they are a student.", "why": "With 이다, use 이기 때문에; 학생 ends in a consonant, so 이 can't drop."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제가 늦은 이유는 길이 ___ 때문이에요.", "answer": "막혔기", "options": ["막혔기", "막혀서", "막힌", "막히고"], "en": "The reason I was late is that the road was jammed.", "why": "End with ~았/었기 때문이에요 to give a past reason."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "요즘 너무 바빠요. ___ 친구를 자주 못 만나요.", "answer": "그렇기 때문에", "options": ["그렇기 때문에", "그렇지만", "그런", "왜냐하면"], "en": "I'm very busy these days. Because of that, I can't see my friends often.", "why": "그렇기 때문에 starts a sentence that follows from the previous one."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "왜 안 먹어요? — 배가 안 ___ 때문이에요.", "answer": "고프기", "options": ["고프기", "고파서", "고픈", "고프게"], "en": "Why aren't you eating? — It's because I'm not hungry.", "why": "~기 때문이에요 answers a 왜 question."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "작년에 주식에 ___ 때문에 지금 돈이 없어요.", "answer": "투자했기", "options": ["투자했기", "투자하기", "투자해서", "투자한"], "en": "I have no money now because I invested in stocks last year.", "why": "The reason is in the past, so use ~았/었기 때문에."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I couldn't sleep because of the noise.", "ko": "소리 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I don't want to do it because it's annoying.", "ko": "귀찮기 때문에 하기 싫어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The streets are crowded because it's the weekend.", "ko": "주말이기 때문에 거리가 북적거려요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "It's because I dropped my phone.", "ko": "휴대폰을 떨어뜨렸기 때문이에요."}], "reading": {"ko": "오늘 아침에 늦게 일어났어요. 어젯밤에 친구하고 오래 통화했기 때문이에요. 늦었기 때문에 세수도 못 했어요. 그리고 버스에서 휴대폰을 떨어뜨렸어요. 그렇기 때문에 지금 휴대폰이 안 돼요. 오늘은 정말 힘든 하루예요.", "en": "I woke up late this morning. It's because I talked on the phone with a friend for a long time last night. Because I was late, I couldn't even wash my face. And I dropped my phone on the bus. Because of that, my phone doesn't work now. Today is a really hard day.", "questions": [{"q": "왜 늦게 일어났어요?", "a": "어젯밤에 친구하고 오래 통화했기 때문이에요."}, {"q": "왜 지금 휴대폰이 안 돼요?", "a": "버스에서 휴대폰을 떨어뜨렸기 때문이에요."}]}}}, {"n": 39, "title": "Lesson 39: Honorific ‘시’ ending, Honorific words", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-39/", "vocab": [{"ko": "자네", "en": "you", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "또래", "en": "age, peer group", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "세제", "en": "laundry detergent", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뒷모습", "en": "appearance from behind", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "봉지", "en": "paper bag, plastic bag", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "국립", "en": "national", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "산소", "en": "oxygen", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "종", "en": "bell", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "주방", "en": "kitchen (restaurant/formal)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "드시다", "en": "to eat (honorific)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "잡수시다", "en": "to eat (very honorific, for elders)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "주무시다", "en": "to sleep (honorific)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "계시다", "en": "to be in/at a location (formal)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "말씀하다", "en": "to speak (honorific)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "드리다", "en": "to give (honorific, to someone)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "소변을 보다", "en": "to urinate", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "대답하다", "en": "to answer, to reply", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "치다", "en": "to tap", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "망치다", "en": "to spoil, to ruin, to screw up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "다가가다", "en": "to approach, to go near", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "다가오다", "en": "to approach, to come near", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "가파르다", "en": "to be steep", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "그저께", "en": "the day before yesterday", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Honorific ~(으)시", "explanation": "Korean shows respect in two separate ways. Endings like ~요 and ~습니다 respect the person you're talking to. ~(으)시 respects the person doing the action, such as a grandparent, teacher, boss or stranger, no matter who you are talking to: even to a friend you say 할머니가 오셨어. Never use it about yourself.\n\nForm: stem + ~시 after a vowel, ~으시 after a consonant, then conjugate. 가다 → 가시 → 가세요, 가셨어요, 가실 거예요. 읽다 → 읽으시 → 읽으세요, 읽으셨어요. 하다 → 하세요. Stems ending in ㄹ drop the ㄹ: 살다 → 사세요, 알다 → 아세요. Irregulars change first: 듣다 → 들으세요, 돕다 → 도우세요.\n\nNote the contractions: 시 + 어요 becomes 세요, and 시 + 었어요 becomes 셨어요. A respected subject can also take ~께서 instead of ~이/가, but ~이/가 is fine in everyday speech.", "examples": [{"ko": "할아버지가 신문을 읽으세요.", "en": "Grandfather is reading the newspaper."}, {"ko": "선생님이 어제 학교에 오셨어요.", "en": "The teacher came to school yesterday."}, {"ko": "할머니는 서울에 사세요.", "en": "Grandmother lives in Seoul."}, {"ko": "아버지가 주방에서 요리하셨어요.", "en": "Father cooked in the kitchen."}, {"ko": "선생님, 내일 학교에 오실 거예요?", "en": "Teacher, will you come to school tomorrow?"}, {"ko": "어머니가 음악을 들으세요.", "en": "Mother is listening to music.", "note": "듣다 is ㄷ irregular: 듣 → 들 + 으세요."}]}, {"point": "Honorific verbs: 드시다, 주무시다, 계시다", "explanation": "A few common verbs don't just add ~(으)시. They have their own honorific word, used whenever the subject deserves respect. 먹다 and 마시다 → 드시다 (잡수시다 is even more respectful, for elders). 자다 → 주무시다. 있다 \"to be (somewhere)\" → 계시다.\n\nThese already contain 시, so conjugate them directly: 드세요, 드셨어요; 주무세요, 주무셨어요; 계세요, 계셨어요. The negative of 계시다 is 안 계시다.\n\nCareful with 있다. 계시다 is only for a respected person being somewhere. For \"to have\", add ~으시 normally: 시간 있으세요? \"Do you have time?\"\n\nMany set phrases use these words: 안녕히 계세요 (goodbye, to someone staying), 안녕히 주무세요 (good night), 맛있게 드세요 (enjoy your meal).", "examples": [{"ko": "할머니가 지금 주무세요.", "en": "Grandmother is sleeping now."}, {"ko": "아버지가 집에 계세요.", "en": "Father is at home."}, {"ko": "할아버지가 저녁을 드셨어요.", "en": "Grandfather ate dinner."}, {"ko": "안녕히 주무세요.", "en": "Good night."}, {"ko": "할머니가 지금 집에 안 계세요.", "en": "Grandmother isn't at home now."}, {"ko": "선생님, 시간 있으세요?", "en": "Teacher, do you have time?", "note": "Here 있다 means \"to have\", so it becomes 있으세요, not 계세요."}]}, {"point": "드리다 and ~께 (to give to someone respected)", "explanation": "When the person receiving something deserves respect, change two things: the verb 주다 becomes 드리다, and the particle ~에게/한테 becomes ~께. 선생님에게 책을 줬어요 → 선생님께 책을 드렸어요.\n\nThe respect goes to the receiver, not to you, so don't add ~시 when you are the giver. Conjugate 드리다 normally: 드리 + 어요 → 드려요, 드렸어요, 드릴 거예요.\n\nWhen a respected person gives something to you, they keep 주다 and add ~시: 할머니가 저에게 책을 주셨어요. ~께 also replaces ~에게 with other verbs: 어머니께 전화를 드렸어요, \"I called my mother\". The subject version of ~께 is ~께서.", "examples": [{"ko": "할아버지께 선물을 드렸어요.", "en": "I gave Grandfather a present."}, {"ko": "선생님께 편지를 드릴 거예요.", "en": "I will give the teacher a letter."}, {"ko": "부모님께 꽃을 드리고 싶어요.", "en": "I want to give my parents flowers."}, {"ko": "할머니가 저에게 책을 주셨어요.", "en": "Grandmother gave me a book."}, {"ko": "어머니께 전화를 드렸어요.", "en": "I called my mother."}, {"ko": "선생님께 뭘 드릴 거예요?", "en": "What are you going to give the teacher?"}]}, {"point": "말씀하다 / 말씀 (to speak, honorific)", "explanation": "말 means \"words, speech\", and 말씀 is its respectful version. 말씀하시다 is the honorific of 말하다 \"to speak\". Use it when a respected person speaks: 말씀하 + 시 + 었어요 → 말씀하셨어요.\n\nThe noun 말씀 has two uses. It can be a respected person's words: 선생님의 말씀. It is also used humbly for your own words to a respected person, with 드리다: 말씀을 드리다 or 말씀드리다, \"to tell (someone respected)\". Here you are lowering yourself, so there is no ~시.\n\nSo don't say 제가 말씀하셨어요 about yourself; say 제가 말씀드렸어요. A useful set phrase when meeting someone: 말씀 많이 들었어요, \"I've heard a lot about you\".", "examples": [{"ko": "교수님이 천천히 말씀하셨어요.", "en": "The professor spoke slowly."}, {"ko": "저는 선생님의 말씀을 잘 들었어요.", "en": "I listened carefully to the teacher’s words."}, {"ko": "할머니께 말씀을 드렸어요.", "en": "I told Grandmother."}, {"ko": "사장님께 그 문제를 말씀드렸어요.", "en": "I told the boss about that problem."}, {"ko": "말씀 많이 들었어요.", "en": "I've heard a lot about you."}, {"ko": "교수님이 언제 말씀하셨어요?", "en": "When did the professor say that?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "할머니가 책을 ___.", "answer": "읽으세요", "options": ["읽으세요", "읽세요", "읽어세요", "읽으시요"], "en": "Grandmother is reading a book.", "why": "읽다 ends in a consonant, so it takes ~으세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님이 어제 한국에서 ___.", "answer": "오셨어요", "options": ["오셨어요", "왔으셨어요", "오셨요", "오시었요"], "en": "The teacher came from Korea yesterday.", "why": "~시 goes before the past tense: 오 + 시 + 었어요 = 오셨어요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아버지는 지금 미국에 ___.", "answer": "사세요", "options": ["사세요", "살으세요", "살세요", "살아세요"], "en": "My father lives in America now.", "why": "ㄹ stems drop the ㄹ before ~시: 살다 becomes 사세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "할아버지가 지금 방에서 ___.", "answer": "주무세요", "options": ["주무세요", "자세요", "자요", "주무어요"], "en": "Grandfather is sleeping in his room now.", "why": "주무시다 is the honorific verb for 자다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님이 지금 교실에 ___.", "answer": "계세요", "options": ["계세요", "있으세요", "있어요", "계시세요"], "en": "The teacher is in the classroom now.", "why": "For a respected person being in a place, use 계시다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님이 점심을 ___.", "answer": "드셨어요", "options": ["드셨어요", "드렸어요", "먹었어요", "드시었요"], "en": "The teacher ate lunch.", "why": "드시다 is the honorific verb for 먹다; 드리다 means to give."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님___ 꽃을 드렸어요.", "answer": "께", "options": ["께", "에게서", "에", "를"], "en": "I gave the teacher flowers.", "why": "~께 is the honorific \"to\" used with 드리다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "할아버지께 커피를 ___ 싶어요.", "answer": "드리고", "options": ["드리고", "주고", "드시고", "주무시고"], "en": "I want to give Grandfather some coffee.", "why": "Giving to a respected person uses 드리다, not 주다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My grandmother lives alone.", "ko": "할머니는 혼자 사세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Is your mother at home?", "ko": "어머니가 집에 계세요?"}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The teacher answered my question.", "ko": "선생님이 제 질문에 대답하셨어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I gave my grandmother a letter.", "ko": "할머니께 편지를 드렸어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "그저께 할머니 집에 갔어요. 할머니는 부산에 사세요. 저는 할머니께 과일을 드렸어요. 할머니가 정말 좋아하셨어요. 저녁에 할머니가 맛있는 음식을 많이 만드셨어요. 할머니는 밤 아홉 시에 주무셨어요.", "en": "The day before yesterday I went to my grandmother's house. My grandmother lives in Busan. I gave her some fruit. She really liked it. In the evening she made a lot of delicious food. She went to sleep at nine at night.", "questions": [{"q": "할머니는 어디에 사세요?", "a": "부산에 사세요."}, {"q": "할머니께 무엇을 드렸어요?", "a": "과일을 드렸어요."}, {"q": "할머니는 몇 시에 주무셨어요?", "a": "아홉 시에 주무셨어요."}]}}}, {"n": 40, "title": "Lesson 40: The Imperative Mood: ~아/어(요), ~(으)세요, ~아/어라, (으)십시오, ~지 말다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-40/", "vocab": [{"ko": "오줌", "en": "urine", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "권력", "en": "power, authority", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "수준", "en": "level, standard", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학력", "en": "level of education", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "직원", "en": "employee, staff member", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "허가", "en": "permission", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "보안", "en": "security (data/systems)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "시선", "en": "one's gaze", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "물체", "en": "object", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "수고하다", "en": "to work hard", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "말다", "en": "to not do (stop)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "직진하다", "en": "to go straight", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "올라가다", "en": "to go up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "올라오다", "en": "to come up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "올려다보다", "en": "to look up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "후들거리다", "en": "to shake, to tremble", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "남용하다", "en": "to abuse", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "감시하다", "en": "to monitor, to watch", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "부끄러워하다", "en": "to be shy (showing it; others)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "슬퍼하다", "en": "to be sad (showing it; others)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "동등하다", "en": "to be equal", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "유쾌하다", "en": "to be pleasant, delightful", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "온순하다", "en": "to be gentle", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "먼저", "en": "first, in advance", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "오른", "en": "right (side)", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "왼", "en": "left (side)", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "줄곧", "en": "constantly, all along", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "가만히", "en": "still, quietly", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Command with ~아/어(요)", "explanation": "The simplest command is the ordinary present tense, said as an instruction with the subject dropped: 와! \"Come!\", 앉아요 \"Sit down.\" Tone and context make it a command.\n\nForm it exactly like the present tense. ㅏ/ㅗ stems → ~아: 앉다 → 앉아, 오다 → 와. Other vowels → ~어: 먹다 → 먹어, 기다리다 → 기다려. 하다 → 해: 직진하다 → 직진해. Irregulars as usual: 듣다 → 들어, 돕다 → 도와.\n\nWithout 요 it is 반말, for friends or younger people. With 요 it is polite but can still sound direct, so with strangers or elders ~(으)세요 is safer. The same form can also be a suggestion: 같이 가요 can mean \"let's go together\".", "examples": [{"ko": "빨리 와!", "en": "Come quickly!"}, {"ko": "여기에 앉아요.", "en": "Sit here."}, {"ko": "다음 사거리에서 직진해.", "en": "Go straight at the next intersection."}, {"ko": "이거 먹어.", "en": "Eat this."}, {"ko": "조금만 기다려.", "en": "Wait a moment."}, {"ko": "이 노래 들어 봐요.", "en": "Try listening to this song.", "note": "듣다 is ㄷ irregular: 듣 + 어 → 들어."}]}, {"point": "Command with ~(으)세요", "explanation": "~(으)세요 is the honorific ~(으)시 plus ~어요. Because it respects the listener, who is also the one doing the action, it is the standard polite way to ask or tell someone to do something: in shops, giving directions, to teachers and strangers.\n\nForm: vowel stem → ~세요 (가다 → 가세요, 쓰다 → 쓰세요). Consonant stem → ~으세요 (앉다 → 앉으세요, 읽다 → 읽으세요). ㄹ stems drop the ㄹ → ~세요 (열다 → 여세요). Honorific verbs use their own forms: 먹다 → 드세요, 자다 → 주무세요, 있다 → 계세요.\n\nThe same form is also a polite statement or question about the listener (lesson 39): 어디 가세요? \"Where are you going?\" Context tells you whether it's a request. Many greetings are commands: 안녕히 가세요 \"go well\", to someone leaving.", "examples": [{"ko": "여기에 앉으세요.", "en": "Please sit here."}, {"ko": "안녕히 가세요.", "en": "Goodbye (go well)."}, {"ko": "이 약을 하루에 세 번 드세요.", "en": "Take this medicine three times a day."}, {"ko": "오른쪽으로 가세요.", "en": "Please go to the right."}, {"ko": "창문을 여세요.", "en": "Please open the window.", "note": "열다 drops ㄹ before ~세요: 열 → 여세요."}, {"ko": "여기에 이름을 쓰세요.", "en": "Please write your name here."}]}, {"point": "Command with ~아/어라", "explanation": "~아/어라 is the plain-style command. Parents use it to children and older people to much younger people. It also appears in written instructions, textbooks and exam questions. Said to an adult who isn't a close friend, it sounds rude.\n\nForm: the ~아/어 present form + 라. ㅏ/ㅗ → ~아라: 앉다 → 앉아라, 자다 → 자라, 일어나다 → 일어나라. Other vowels → ~어라: 먹다 → 먹어라. 하다 → 해라: 공부하다 → 공부해라. Irregulars: 듣다 → 들어라, 돕다 → 도와라.\n\nCompare the 반말 command ~아/어 (먹어!). Both are casual, but ~아/어라 sounds stronger, like an order from above.", "examples": [{"ko": "빨리 일어나라!", "en": "Get up quickly!"}, {"ko": "숙제를 먼저 해라.", "en": "Do your homework first."}, {"ko": "가만히 있어라.", "en": "Stay still."}, {"ko": "밥을 많이 먹어라.", "en": "Eat a lot."}, {"ko": "일찍 자라.", "en": "Go to bed early."}, {"ko": "선생님 말씀을 잘 들어라.", "en": "Listen carefully to what your teacher says."}]}, {"point": "Command with ~(으)십시오", "explanation": "~(으)십시오 is the most formal command. It combines honorific ~(으)시 with the formal style you know from ~ㅂ니다/습니다. You hear it in subway announcements, on signs, in hotels and banks, and from staff to customers. In normal conversation it can sound stiff, so ~(으)세요 is more common.\n\nForm: vowel stem → ~십시오 (가다 → 가십시오, 기다리다 → 기다리십시오). Consonant stem → ~으십시오 (앉다 → 앉으십시오). ㄹ stems drop the ㄹ (열다 → 여십시오). Honorific verbs: 드십시오, 계십시오.\n\nIt is spelled 시오, though it often sounds like 시요. The negative is ~지 마십시오 (next point).", "examples": [{"ko": "여기에 앉으십시오.", "en": "Please be seated here."}, {"ko": "안으로 들어오십시오.", "en": "Please come inside."}, {"ko": "잠시만 기다리십시오.", "en": "Please wait a moment."}, {"ko": "안녕히 가십시오.", "en": "Goodbye (go well)."}, {"ko": "문을 여십시오.", "en": "Please open the door."}, {"ko": "맛있게 드십시오.", "en": "Please enjoy your meal."}]}, {"point": "Negative command ~지 말다", "explanation": "You can't make a \"don't\" command with 안 or ~지 않다. Instead Korean uses the verb 말다 \"to stop, not do\". Attach ~지 to the verb stem, then put the command ending on 말다.\n\nCasual: ~지 마 (가지 마). Polite: ~지 마세요 (먹지 마세요). Plain: ~지 마라 (늦지 마라). Formal: ~지 마십시오 (만지지 마십시오). 말다 is an ㄹ verb, so it shortens: 말 + 세요 → 마세요, 말아 → 마.\n\nThe stem before ~지 never changes, even for irregulars: 듣다 → 듣지 마세요. A common mistake is 안 가세요 for \"don't go\". That actually means \"you don't go\" (a statement). Use 가지 마세요.", "examples": [{"ko": "여기에서 담배를 피우지 마세요.", "en": "Please don’t smoke here."}, {"ko": "걱정하지 마.", "en": "Don’t worry."}, {"ko": "그 물체를 만지지 마십시오.", "en": "Do not touch that object."}, {"ko": "늦지 마라.", "en": "Don’t be late."}, {"ko": "아직 가지 마세요.", "en": "Please don't go yet."}, {"ko": "그 사람 말을 듣지 마.", "en": "Don't listen to that person.", "note": "The stem stays the same before ~지: 듣지, not 들지."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "의자에 ___.", "answer": "앉으세요", "options": ["앉으세요", "앉세요", "앉아세요", "앉으요"], "en": "Please sit on the chair.", "why": "앉다 ends in a consonant, so the polite command is 앉으세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "앞으로 ___.", "answer": "직진하십시오", "options": ["직진하십시오", "직진하십시요", "직진합십시오", "직진하시십시오"], "en": "Please go straight ahead. (very formal)", "why": "The most formal command is ~(으)십시오, spelled with 오."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "방을 깨끗하게 ___.", "answer": "청소해라", "options": ["청소해라", "청소하어라", "청소해러", "청소했라"], "en": "Clean your room! (plain command to a child)", "why": "~아/어라 is the blunt plain command; 하다 becomes 해라."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "늦지 ___.", "answer": "마", "options": ["마", "말", "마서", "말고"], "en": "Don't be late. (casual)", "why": "~지 말다 becomes ~지 마 as a casual command."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "여기에서 음식을 먹지 ___.", "answer": "마세요", "options": ["마세요", "말세요", "말으세요", "마서요"], "en": "Please don't eat food here.", "why": "말다 drops its ㄹ before ~세요: 마세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "빨리 ___!", "answer": "일어나", "options": ["일어나", "일어너", "일어나아", "일어났어"], "en": "Get up quickly! (casual)", "why": "The casual command is the plain ~아/어 form; 일어나 + 아 contracts to 일어나."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "안에서 담배를 ___ 마십시오.", "answer": "피우지", "options": ["피우지", "피우고", "피워", "피우게"], "en": "Do not smoke inside.", "why": "Negative commands are verb stem + ~지 + a form of 말다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "왼쪽으로 ___.", "answer": "가세요", "options": ["가세요", "가으세요", "갈세요", "가새요"], "en": "Please go to the left.", "why": "가다 ends in a vowel, so it takes ~세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please don't worry.", "ko": "걱정하지 마세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please come up here.", "ko": "여기로 올라오세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Wash your hands first. (to a child)", "ko": "먼저 손을 씻어라."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please do not go in. (formal sign)", "ko": "들어가지 마십시오."}], "reading": {"ko": "엄마가 아이에게 편지를 썼어요. 일찍 일어나라. 일어나서 먼저 세수해라. 그리고 아침을 꼭 먹어라. 학교에서 친구하고 다투지 마라. 선생님 말씀을 잘 들어라. 늦지 마라!", "en": "Mom wrote a letter to her child. Get up early. When you get up, wash your face first. And be sure to eat breakfast. Don't argue with your friends at school. Listen carefully to your teacher. Don't be late!", "questions": [{"q": "누가 편지를 썼어요?", "a": "엄마가 썼어요."}, {"q": "아이는 일어나서 먼저 뭐 해요?", "a": "세수해요."}]}}}, {"n": 41, "title": "Lesson 41: To Give: 주다, ~아/어 주다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-34-41/lesson-41/", "vocab": [{"ko": "동료", "en": "colleague, peer", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사거리", "en": "intersection", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "어린이집", "en": "day care", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "유치원", "en": "kindergarten", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뿌리", "en": "roots", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "무역", "en": "trade", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "턱", "en": "chin", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "왼발", "en": "left foot", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "왼손", "en": "left hand", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오른발", "en": "right foot", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "오른손", "en": "right hand", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "손바닥", "en": "palm", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "음성", "en": "voice (audio, e.g. voice message)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이마", "en": "forehead", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "발가락", "en": "toe", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "발목", "en": "ankle", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "엉덩이", "en": "butt", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "신체", "en": "body (formal/medical)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "감옥", "en": "prison", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "볼", "en": "cheek", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "제자리", "en": "one's original place", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "상대하다", "en": "to deal with (people)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "미치다", "en": "to go crazy", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "멀리", "en": "far, far away", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "너무나", "en": "extremely", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "최초", "en": "the very first (ever)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "초", "en": "beginning of a period", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "말", "en": "end of a period", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "결코", "en": "never (with negatives)", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "주다 with commands (give me)", "explanation": "주다 means \"to give\". To ask for something, put a command ending on 주다 (lesson 40): 줘 (casual, 주 + 어 → 줘), 주세요 (polite), 주십시오 (formal). You don't need to say \"me\"; it's understood. To say \"give it to him\", add the person with ~에게/한테: 동생한테 줘.\n\nThis is how you order and shop: name the thing, then 주세요. Counters go after the noun: 사과 두 개 주세요. Particles like ~을/를 are often dropped.\n\n좀 \"a little\" softens the request, a bit like \"please\": 물 좀 주세요. Remember the respect rules: you give to a respected person with 드리다, and they give to you with 주시다 (lesson 39).", "examples": [{"ko": "물 좀 주세요.", "en": "Please give me some water."}, {"ko": "그 책을 줘.", "en": "Give me that book."}, {"ko": "사과 두 개 주세요.", "en": "Two apples, please."}, {"ko": "동생한테 이 사탕을 줘.", "en": "Give this candy to your younger sibling."}, {"ko": "여권을 주십시오.", "en": "Please give me your passport."}, {"ko": "그 우산 좀 줘요.", "en": "Give me that umbrella, please."}]}, {"point": "~아/어 주다 (do something for someone)", "explanation": "Attach ~아/어 주다 to a verb when the action is done for someone's benefit: a favor, help or kindness. 만들다 \"to make\" → 만들어 주다 \"to make for someone\".\n\nForm the first verb like the present tense without 요, then add 주다. ㅏ/ㅗ → ~아 주다: 돕다 → 도와 주다, 오다 → 와 주다. Other vowels → ~어 주다: 열다 → 열어 주다, 찍다 → 찍어 주다. 하다 → 해 주다. 보이다 → 보여 주다 \"to show\". 주다 then takes the tense and politeness: 열어 줬어요, 열어 줄 거예요, 열어 주세요.\n\nWith a command ending it becomes a polite request, softer than ~(으)세요 alone because you are asking a favor. Respect rules still apply: a respected person does it for you with ~아/어 주시다 (만들어 주셨어요); you do it for them with ~아/어 드리다 (읽어 드렸어요).", "examples": [{"ko": "엄마가 도시락을 만들어 주셨어요.", "en": "Mom made a lunch box for me."}, {"ko": "동료가 제 일을 도와 줬어요.", "en": "My colleague helped me with my work."}, {"ko": "창문 좀 열어 주세요.", "en": "Please open the window."}, {"ko": "그 사진을 보여 주세요.", "en": "Please show me that picture."}, {"ko": "친구가 제 사진을 찍어 줬어요.", "en": "My friend took a picture of me."}, {"ko": "할머니께 책을 읽어 드렸어요.", "en": "I read a book to Grandmother.", "note": "For a respected person, 주다 becomes 드리다: 읽어 드리다."}]}, {"point": "~지 말아 주세요 (please don’t)", "explanation": "This joins two patterns: ~지 말다 \"don't\" (lesson 40) and ~아/어 주세요 \"please do for me\". 말다 + ~아 주세요 → 말아 주세요. So 떠들지 말아 주세요 is literally \"please do me the favor of not making noise\".\n\nForm: stem + ~지 말아 주세요, and the stem doesn't change: 가다 → 가지 말아 주세요, 잊다 → 잊지 말아 주세요. Here 말다 keeps its full form 말아; it does not shorten to 마.\n\nIt is softer and more pleading than ~지 마세요, so it suits notices, requests to strangers and asking favors. The casual version is ~지 말아 줘, and the formal one is ~지 말아 주십시오.", "examples": [{"ko": "여기에서 떠들지 말아 주세요.", "en": "Please don’t make noise here."}, {"ko": "제 방에 들어오지 말아 주세요.", "en": "Please don’t come into my room."}, {"ko": "내일은 늦지 말아 주세요.", "en": "Please don’t be late tomorrow."}, {"ko": "저를 잊지 말아 주세요.", "en": "Please don't forget me."}, {"ko": "걱정하지 말아 줘.", "en": "Please don't worry."}, {"ko": "사진을 찍지 말아 주십시오.", "en": "Please do not take photos."}]}, {"point": "~아/어 줘서 고마워요 (thank you for ...)", "explanation": "To thank someone for doing something, chain three pieces: the verb + ~아/어 주다 (they did it for you), then ~아/어서 \"because\" (lesson 37), then 고맙다 or 감사하다. 주 + 어서 → 줘서. So 오다 → 와 주다 → 와 줘서 고마워요, \"thank you for coming\".\n\nWith someone you respect, add ~시: 주시 + 어서 → 주셔서, and use 감사합니다: 도와 주셔서 감사합니다. Choose the level: 고마워 (friends), 고마워요 (polite), 감사합니다 (respectful).\n\nEven though the favor already happened, there is no past tense before ~아/어서: say 와 줘서, not 와 줬어서.", "examples": [{"ko": "와 줘서 고마워요.", "en": "Thank you for coming."}, {"ko": "도와 주셔서 감사합니다.", "en": "Thank you for helping me."}, {"ko": "제 말을 들어 줘서 고마워.", "en": "Thanks for listening to me."}, {"ko": "저녁을 요리해 줘서 고마워요.", "en": "Thank you for cooking dinner."}, {"ko": "선물을 보내 주셔서 감사합니다.", "en": "Thank you for sending a present."}, {"ko": "기다려 줘서 고마워.", "en": "Thanks for waiting."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "문 좀 ___ 주세요.", "answer": "닫아", "options": ["닫아", "닫어", "닫고", "닫은"], "en": "Please close the door.", "why": "닫다 has ㅏ, so it takes ~아 before 주세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "표 좀 ___ 주세요.", "answer": "보여", "options": ["보여", "봐", "보이", "보고"], "en": "Please show me your ticket.", "why": "보여 주다 means \"to show\"; 봐 주세요 would mean \"please look at\"."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아버지가 저에게 자전거를 사 ___.", "answer": "주셨어요", "options": ["주셨어요", "드렸어요", "드리셨어요", "주시었요"], "en": "My father bought me a bicycle.", "why": "A respected person doing a favor for me: ~아/어 주시다; 드리다 is only for giving to them."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "친구가 이사를 ___ 줬어요.", "answer": "도와", "options": ["도와", "돕아", "도와서", "돕고"], "en": "My friend helped me move.", "why": "돕다 is ㅂ irregular and becomes 도와 before 주다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 컴퓨터를 만지지 ___ 주세요.", "answer": "말아", "options": ["말아", "마", "마세요", "말고"], "en": "Please don't touch my computer.", "why": "\"Please don't\" as a favor is ~지 말아 주세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "생일 선물을 ___ 고마워요.", "answer": "줘서", "options": ["줘서", "주고", "줬어서", "주세요"], "en": "Thank you for giving me a birthday present.", "why": "Thanking someone uses ~아/어서 + 고마워요, with no past tense before 서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "기다려 ___ 감사합니다.", "answer": "주셔서", "options": ["주셔서", "드려서", "주셨어서", "주시고"], "en": "Thank you for waiting. (to a respected person)", "why": "With respected people, use ~아/어 주셔서 감사합니다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 펜 좀 ___.", "answer": "줘", "options": ["줘", "주", "줘서", "주고"], "en": "Give me that pen. (casual)", "why": "The casual command of 주다 is 줘."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please show me the menu.", "ko": "메뉴 좀 보여 주세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Thanks for listening to me. (casual)", "ko": "내 말을 들어 줘서 고마워."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please don't open my bag.", "ko": "제 가방을 열지 말아 주세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My colleague bought me coffee.", "ko": "동료가 커피를 사 줬어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "어제 저는 왼발 발목을 다쳤어요. 그래서 걷기가 아주 힘들었어요. 동료가 제 가방을 들어 줬어요. 친구는 저녁을 만들어 줬어요. 저는 두 사람에게 정말 고마웠어요. 오늘은 동료에게 커피를 사 줄 거예요.", "en": "Yesterday I hurt my left ankle. So walking was very hard. My colleague carried my bag for me. My friend made dinner for me. I was really grateful to both of them. Today I'm going to buy my colleague a coffee.", "questions": [{"q": "어디를 다쳤어요?", "a": "왼발 발목을 다쳤어요."}, {"q": "동료가 무엇을 해 줬어요?", "a": "가방을 들어 줬어요."}, {"q": "오늘 동료에게 무엇을 사 줄 거예요?", "a": "커피를 사 줄 거예요."}]}}}, {"n": 42, "title": "Lesson 42: When one does: ~ㄹ/을 때", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-2-lower-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-2-lessons-42-50/lesson-42/", "vocab": [{"ko": "레인", "en": "lane", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뼈", "en": "bone", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뼈마디", "en": "joint", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "도매", "en": "wholesale", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소매", "en": "retail", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사인", "en": "autograph", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뇌", "en": "brain", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "집안", "en": "inside a house", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "배경", "en": "background", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "복권", "en": "lottery ticket", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "시간표", "en": "timetable", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사막", "en": "desert", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "곤충", "en": "bug, insect", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "버티다", "en": "to hold out", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "견디다", "en": "to withstand", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "응원하다", "en": "to cheer on, to root for", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "응시하다", "en": "to gaze, to look", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "짐작하다", "en": "to guess", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "죽이다", "en": "to kill", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "감다", "en": "to wash one's hair", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "예의가 바르다", "en": "to be polite", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "얕다", "en": "to be shallow", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "어쨌든", "en": "at any rate, in any case, anyways", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "깊이", "en": "deeply", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "겨우", "en": "barely, hardly, narrowly", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "가짜", "en": "fake", "pos": "noun"}], "grammar": [{"point": "Noun + 때 (at the time of N)", "explanation": "때 means \"time\" in the sense of \"the time when\". Put it after a noun that names an event or period: 방학 때 \"during vacation\", 점심 때 \"at lunchtime\", 휴가 때, 시험 때, 생일 때. You usually don't need ~에 after it. 그때 means \"at that time, back then\", pointing to a time already mentioned.\n\nYou can add ~는 for contrast or ~도 for \"also\": 그때는, 생일 때도.\n\nDon't use 때 with words that already name a time on the calendar or clock, like days, dates and hours. Those take ~에: 월요일에, 3시에, never 월요일 때. 때 is for events and periods: 결혼식 때, 추석 때.", "examples": [{"ko": "방학 때 한국에 갈 거예요.", "en": "I will go to Korea during vacation."}, {"ko": "점심 때 도시락을 먹었어요.", "en": "I ate my lunch box at lunchtime."}, {"ko": "그때 저는 학생이었어요.", "en": "At that time, I was a student."}, {"ko": "휴가 때 뭐 할 거예요?", "en": "What are you going to do on your vacation?"}, {"ko": "생일 때 친구들이 선물을 줬어요.", "en": "My friends gave me presents on my birthday."}, {"ko": "그때는 한국어를 전혀 몰랐어요.", "en": "Back then, I didn't know Korean at all."}]}, {"point": "~(으)ㄹ 때 (when ...)", "explanation": "Attach ~(으)ㄹ 때 to a verb or adjective to say \"when (something happens or is the case)\". It is the future modifier ~(으)ㄹ (lesson 26) + 때 \"time\", but it isn't only about the future. It works for habits, general truths and future events, and the final verb sets the tense. The main clause happens during that time. ~에 after 때 is usually dropped.\n\nVowel stem → ~ㄹ 때: 공부하다 → 공부할 때, 피곤하다 → 피곤할 때. Consonant stem → ~을 때: 먹다 → 먹을 때. ㄹ stem → no change: 살다 → 살 때. Irregulars: 춥다 → 추울 때, 듣다 → 들을 때. With 이다: 학생일 때.\n\nFor an action that was already finished at that moment, use ~았/었을 때 instead (next point).", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 공부할 때 음악을 들어요.", "en": "I listen to music when I study."}, {"ko": "밥을 먹을 때 텔레비전을 보지 마세요.", "en": "Don’t watch TV while you eat."}, {"ko": "피곤할 때 커피를 마셔요.", "en": "I drink coffee when I’m tired."}, {"ko": "머리를 감을 때 노래를 불러요.", "en": "I sing when I wash my hair."}, {"ko": "날씨가 추울 때 이 코트를 입어요.", "en": "I wear this coat when it's cold.", "note": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular: 춥 → 추우 + ㄹ → 추울."}, {"ko": "한국에 살 때 김치를 많이 먹었어요.", "en": "When I lived in Korea, I ate a lot of kimchi."}]}, {"point": "~았/었을 때 (when ... happened)", "explanation": "To talk about a moment in the past, especially a finished action, put the past ~았/었 before ~을 때. The past form always ends in ㅆ, so it is always ~을, never ~ㄹ: 가다 → 갔을 때, 먹다 → 먹었을 때, 하다 → 했을 때, 어리다 → 어렸을 때 \"when I was young\".\n\n~(으)ㄹ 때 vs ~았/었을 때: use ~았/었을 때 when the action was complete at that moment. 집에 갈 때 is \"when I was going home (on the way)\", while 집에 갔을 때 is \"when I got home\".\n\nFor past states like being young or being a student, ~았/었을 때 is the usual choice: 어렸을 때, 학생이었을 때.", "examples": [{"ko": "한국에 있었을 때 한국어를 많이 배웠어요.", "en": "I learned a lot of Korean when I was in Korea."}, {"ko": "어렸을 때 저는 곤충을 무서워했어요.", "en": "When I was young, I was scared of insects."}, {"ko": "집에 도착했을 때 아무도 없었어요.", "en": "When I arrived home, nobody was there."}, {"ko": "학생이었을 때 매일 도서관에 갔어요.", "en": "When I was a student, I went to the library every day."}, {"ko": "처음 김치를 먹었을 때 너무 매웠어요.", "en": "When I first ate kimchi, it was too spicy."}, {"ko": "그 소식을 들었을 때 어땠어요?", "en": "How did you feel when you heard the news?"}]}, {"point": "때 with other particles", "explanation": "때 is a noun, so other particles can attach to it, after a noun or after a ~(으)ㄹ 때 clause. The particle goes right after 때 and the sentence continues normally.\n\n때까지 \"until\": 친구가 올 때까지. 때부터 \"since, from when\": 어렸을 때부터. 때마다 \"every time, whenever\" (~마다 from lesson 25): 들을 때마다. 때는 \"as for when\", for contrast: 비가 올 때는 집에 있어요 (but other times I go out). 때도 \"even when, also when\": 아플 때도.", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 어렸을 때부터 축구를 좋아했어요.", "en": "I have liked soccer since I was young."}, {"ko": "그 노래를 들을 때마다 행복해요.", "en": "Every time I hear that song, I’m happy."}, {"ko": "비가 올 때는 집에 있어요.", "en": "When it rains, I stay home."}, {"ko": "친구가 올 때까지 기다릴 거예요.", "en": "I will wait until my friend comes."}, {"ko": "아플 때도 회사에 갔어요.", "en": "I went to work even when I was sick."}, {"ko": "한국에 갈 때마다 친구를 만나요.", "en": "Every time I go to Korea, I meet my friend."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 운동___ 때 음악을 들어요.", "answer": "할", "options": ["할", "하는", "한", "해"], "en": "I listen to music when I exercise.", "why": "Verb stem + ~(으)ㄹ 때 means \"when\"; 하다 becomes 할."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "책을 ___ 때 안경을 써요.", "answer": "읽을", "options": ["읽을", "읽는", "읽은", "읽울"], "en": "I wear glasses when I read books.", "why": "읽다 ends in a consonant, so it takes ~을 때."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 때 저는 부산에 살았어요.", "answer": "어렸을", "options": ["어렸을", "어린", "어렸는", "어려운"], "en": "When I was young, I lived in Busan.", "why": "For a time in the past, use ~았/었을 때: 어렸을 때."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제가 역에 ___ 때 친구가 기다리고 있었어요.", "answer": "도착했을", "options": ["도착했을", "도착한", "도착하는", "도착했는"], "en": "When I arrived at the station, my friend was waiting.", "why": "A completed past action takes ~았/었을 때."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "점심 ___ 뭐 먹었어요?", "answer": "때", "options": ["때", "을 때", "때문에", "에서"], "en": "What did you eat at lunchtime?", "why": "A noun takes 때 directly, with no ~을."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 사진을 ___ 때마다 웃어요.", "answer": "볼", "options": ["볼", "보는", "본", "봐"], "en": "Every time I look at this photo, I smile.", "why": "때마다 follows the ~(으)ㄹ form: 볼 때마다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "버스가 올 때___ 여기서 기다리세요.", "answer": "까지", "options": ["까지", "부터", "마다", "에서"], "en": "Please wait here until the bus comes.", "why": "때까지 means \"until\"."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 초등학교 때___ 한국 음악을 좋아했어요.", "answer": "부터", "options": ["부터", "까지", "마다", "에서"], "en": "I have liked Korean music since elementary school.", "why": "때부터 means \"since\" that time."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "When I'm sad, I listen to music.", "ko": "슬플 때 음악을 들어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "When I was in Korea, I ate a lot of kimchi.", "ko": "한국에 있었을 때 김치를 많이 먹었어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I will go to Busan during vacation.", "ko": "방학 때 부산에 갈 거예요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Every time I go to Seoul, I meet my friend.", "ko": "서울에 갈 때마다 친구를 만나요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 어렸을 때 곤충을 아주 무서워했어요. 곤충을 볼 때마다 울었어요. 초등학교 때 선생님이 곤충에 관한 책을 주셨어요. 그때부터 곤충을 좋아하기 시작했어요. 지금은 시간이 있을 때 산에 가서 곤충을 봐요. 곤충은 정말 신기한 것 같아요.", "en": "When I was young, I was very scared of insects. Every time I saw an insect, I cried. In elementary school, a teacher gave me a book about insects. From then on, I started to like insects. Now when I have time, I go to the mountains and look at insects. I think insects are really amazing.", "questions": [{"q": "어렸을 때 무엇을 무서워했어요?", "a": "곤충을 무서워했어요."}, {"q": "누가 곤충에 관한 책을 주셨어요?", "a": "선생님이 주셨어요."}, {"q": "지금은 시간이 있을 때 뭐 해요?", "a": "산에 가서 곤충을 봐요."}]}}}]);
